Java 5 API index, used to verify the BD-J jars
==============================================

apple-jdk-1.5.0_19.txt.gz lists what Apple's Java 1.5.0_19 declares: class
names, the class hierarchy, and member name/descriptor pairs. It is what
contrib/src/bluray/check-bdj-java5.py resolves the BD-J jars against, so the
contrib build fails if the jars reference anything Mac OS X 10.4 / 10.5 cannot
provide. See contrib/src/bluray/rules.mak for why the jars target Java 5.

Why an index and not a class library
------------------------------------

contrib/java6-bootclasspath holds OpenJDK 6's rt.jar outright, because OpenJDK 6
is GPLv2 with the Classpath exception. There is no equivalent for Java 5:

  - OpenJDK started at Java 6. Sun never open-sourced JDK 5.
  - Apache Harmony (Apache 2.0) and GNU Classpath (GPL + linking exception) do
    cover the Java 5 API, but they reimplement the platform and carry none of
    Sun's internals. The BD-J adaptation layer plugs directly into those
    (java.io.FileSystem, java.awt.peer.*, sun.awt.*), so checking against
    either would report a flood of absent classes that say nothing about
    whether the jars run on a real Mac OS X JRE.
  - Apple's own class library answers the question exactly, but is not
    redistributable.

So the implementation is not copied: only the list of names it declares is,
which is the same shape as the per-release API data a JDK ships in ct.sym.
1.1 MB against 28 MB of jars, and nothing that runs.

Regenerating
------------

Point the generator at a Mac OS X 10.4 or 10.5 install:

    JC=/System/Library/Frameworks/JavaVM.framework/Versions/1.5/Classes
    python3 contrib/src/bluray/make-java5-api-index.py \
        -o contrib/java5-api/apple-jdk-1.5.0_19.txt.gz \
        --source "Apple Java 1.5.0_19 (build 1.5.0_19-b02-306), Mac OS X 10.4.11 PowerPC" \
        $JC/classes.jar $JC/ui.jar $JC/charsets.jar $JC/jce.jar $JC/jsse.jar

classes.jar covers almost everything; ui.jar matters because it holds the AWT
peer and look-and-feel classes the BD-J adaptation layer sits on top of. The
output is deterministic, so regenerating an unchanged index leaves the file
byte-identical.

check-bdj-java5.py also still accepts --runtime <jar> to check straight against
a real JRE's class library, when one happens to be at hand.
